                        LITERATURE SURVEY
 

CHAPTER II 

2.1 Key Challenges and Research Gaps 

A literature survey helps to understand existing technologies, applications, and research related to pharmaceutical information systems. Various digital healthcare platforms provide medicine information, online purchasing, and healthcare services. However, many existing systems do not provide all the features required to make medicine information easily accessible to elderly people, rural populations, and non-English-speaking users. 

The DawaiSamjho project aims to address the following challenges and research gaps: 

1. Difficulty in Medicine Identification 

Many users find it difficult to identify medicines from printed names on medicine strips. Existing manual search methods require users to type the correct medicine name. OCR technology can simplify this process by extracting text directly from medicine packaging. 

2. Lack of Multilingual Support 

Most pharmaceutical platforms provide information mainly in English. People who understand Hindi better may face difficulties understanding medicine instructions. DawaiSamjho aims to provide simplified information in both Hindi and English. 

3. Limited Voice Assistance 

Elderly and visually impaired users may experience difficulties reading small printed text. Voice-based assistance using Text-to-Speech technology can make medicine information easier to access. 

4. High Cost of Branded Medicines 

Many consumers are unaware of affordable generic alternatives containing equivalent active ingredients. DawaiSamjho aims to provide a comparison between branded and generic medicines to increase awareness of affordable treatment options. 

5. Duplicate Salt Identification 

Different branded medicines may contain the same active pharmaceutical ingredients. Taking duplicate ingredients unknowingly may create health risks. The proposed system aims to identify duplicate salts and provide appropriate alerts. 

6. Drug Interaction Awareness 

Taking multiple medicines together may cause potentially harmful interactions. Existing medicine information systems may not provide an integrated interaction-checking facility. DawaiSamjho aims to include a database-based drug interaction checking module. 

 

 

 

 

 

 

 

 

 

